# Line endings and diff behaviour.
#
# This repo is authored on Windows but half of it runs on Linux: the Hugging Face Spaces in
# resources/*-space/ build from Dockerfiles, and scripts/finetune/*.sh are shipped to a rented
# GPU box. A CRLF that reaches either of those is not a cosmetic problem — `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`
# fails with "bad interpreter: no such file or directory", which is a confusing way to lose an
# afternoon. Normalising in the repo is what stops that happening.

# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository. Git still checks out CRLF on Windows
# where core.autocrlf says to; what this guarantees is that the committed blob is LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must be LF on disk even on Windows, because something other than Windows reads them.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts, conversely, must keep CRLF — cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only .bat files.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Explicitly binary. Without this git may try to normalise or diff them, which corrupts the
# file and produces useless multi-megabyte diffs in review.
*.mp4           binary
*.mp3           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.onnx          binary
*.joblib        binary
*.pkl           binary
*.xlsx          binary
*.sqlite3       binary
*.gguf          binary
*.safetensors   binary

# GitHub language statistics. Without these the repo reports as whatever the vendored code and
# the generated lockfiles happen to be, rather than what anyone actually maintains here.
backend/vendor/**       linguist-vendored
package-lock.json       linguist-generated
requirements.lock.txt   linguist-generated

# Docs shouldn't count toward the language bar, but should still render.
docs/**                 linguist-documentation
